<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Servius Tullius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">trad. r. 578–535 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Servius Tullius was, according to Roman tradition, the sixth king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, reigning in the 6th century BCE. He was credited with the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, the division of citizens into wealth classes that formed the basis of the army and the centuriate assembly, and the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servian-wall">Servian Wall</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> identified him with the Etruscan adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastarna">Mastarna</a> of Vulci, shown in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-tomb">François Tomb</a> paintings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/servius-tullius.jpg" alt="Servius Tullius" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille(1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servius_by_Rouille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
